Sure! Here's a simple quicksort in Python. It picks a pivot, splits the list into the items smaller than the pivot, equal to it and larger than it, and sorts the two outer parts the same way until they're empty:

def quicksort(items):
    if len(items) <= 1:
        return items
    pivot = items[len(items) // 2]
    left = [x for x in items if x < pivot]
    middle = [x for x in items if x == pivot]
    right = [x for x in items if x > pivot]
    return quicksort(left) + middle + quicksort(right)

It runs in O(n log n) time on average, but O(n²) in the worst case, when the pivot is always the smallest or largest item. Picking a random pivot makes that unlikely. Let me know if you'd like an in-place version instead!
